---
/**
 * /route: the route planner. The island (RoutePlanner.tsx) computes everything in the browser
 * from this site's own data files; this page adds the explanation that works without
 * JavaScript.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import LabelChip from '../components/LabelChip.astro';
import DataBadge from '../components/DataBadge.astro';
import RoutePlanner from '../components/route/RoutePlanner.tsx';
import { pageHref } from '../lib/paths';
import { REPO_URL } from '../lib/site';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Route planner"
  description="Pick two cities and a time: your browser plans a path over today's real Starlink fleet on modelled laser links and writes it, with per-hop deadlines, into a T-CRH header you can download as a pcap."
>
  <header class="page-hero page-hero--compact container">
    <p class="eyebrow">Route planner</p>
    <h1>Plan a path, see the bytes</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Pick two cities and a time within six hours of the orbit snapshot. Your browser computes where every satellite in
      the orbital planes of Starlink's 53° / 463 km shell is, plans the shortest path over our model of the laser links,
      gives every hop a deadline, and writes the result into the T-CRH routing header, byte for byte.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="section section--flush" aria-label="Route planner">
    <div class="container">
      <noscript>
        <p class="noscript">
          The route planner computes in your browser and needs JavaScript. The same computation runs from the command line:
          <code>cr route --from Chennai --to London</code> (see the <a href={REPO_URL}>repository</a>).
        </p>
      </noscript>
      <RoutePlanner client:load />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="how-title">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="section__head">
        <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
        <h2 id="how-title">The same rules as the Python reference</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The planner is a TypeScript port of the project's Python router (<code>cr route</code>). For the chosen instant it
          builds the network of the shell: the laser links of our Grid-4 model <LabelChip kind="modelled" size="sm" note="laser links" />
          that are up at that instant, and each city's links to the four highest satellites at least 25° above its horizon
          <LabelChip kind="modelled" size="sm" note="ground links" />. Every link weighs its light time rounded to the nearest
          nanosecond plus 0.5 ms of processing, so all costs are whole numbers and every implementation adds them up the same
          way. Dijkstra's algorithm finds the cheapest path; when two paths cost exactly the same, the lower node number wins.
        </p>
        <p>
          Each hop then gets a budget, the slack S (1.5 by default) times its predicted delay, and the cumulative budgets
          become the deadlines in the header, rounded up to whole deadline units (0.1 ms by default). If a delta exceeds
          one byte, it is clamped and SAT is set, as the specification's encoding rule requires. The header bytes come from the site's T-CRH engine, which passes every shared
          golden test vector. <LabelChip kind="real" size="sm" note="header bytes" />
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>Checked against Python.</strong> On every deployment, CI runs the Python router and this planner on the same
          satellite and city positions for 20 fixed city pairs, twice: on the project's reference snapshot and on the
          snapshot this page serves. It stops the deployment if any path cost differs by more than 1 ns or any byte of the
          header, packet or capture file differs. Two different paths whose costs agree within 1 ns are allowed, reported as
          ties (never as identical), and their header, packet and capture file are still compared on Python's path. Positions themselves are compared separately for both snapshots, using the published element sets for the served snapshot: the browser's SGP4 library keeps element epochs to the
          millisecond only, so its positions differ from Python's by up to about 8 metres, which is why the route check
          uses one shared set of positions.
        </p>
        <p>
          What this is not: Starlink's routing. It is not public, and Starlink does not use T-CRH.
          <LabelChip kind="unknown" size="sm" note="Starlink's routing" /> The modelling choices are listed on the
          <a href={pageHref('methodology')}>methodology page</a>; the header format on the <a href={pageHref('protocol')}>protocol page</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-label="Orbit snapshot">
    <div class="container">
      <DataBadge />
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-hero--compact {
    padding-block: var(--space-6) var(--space-5);
  }

  .page-hero--compact h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    max-width: none;
  }

  .page-hero--compact .lede {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    max-width: 75ch;
  }

  .noscript {
    padding: var(--space-4);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: var(--radius-2);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
</style>
